From 94843c11fab57bc68a175c3c741363b45a129046 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:44:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDDemuxBluray3D: after a picture that went on alone, the
 second view waits for an anchor

Appending resumed wherever the two views met again: in the middle of a group of pictures,
after pictures of the base view had gone on alone. Every picture of the second view from
there to the next anchor was then decoded from a reference that the decoder did not have,
and came out wrong without a word.

- With a second view open, every way out of Read() without it counts as a picture that went
  on alone: no second view met it, it has no time, the clip was given up, or the two could
  not be put together. From then on the access units of the second view are read and dropped
  until one that is an anchor picture, and those that are shown before that anchor are dropped
  too (KODI::MULTIVIEW::Rejoin). After 300 pictures without an anchor the second view is
  appended again as it comes. The first pictures of a clip and those after a seek or a flush
  are appended as they come, as before.
- The mark is asked for before every packet that is put together, and nothing is appended
  where the stream cannot be marked: "nothing is appended to a stream that is not marked" by
  construction.
- The line of the mark says what the demuxer did (the stream is marked, the second view is
  appended), not what a decoder will do; the comment where the change of the stream is
  counted says when the player does not act on it.
- A clip whose second view does not meet the base view is placed again three times without a
  meeting in between, and then left alone until a seek, a flush or another clip. It was
  placed again every five seconds for as long as it played.
- The eye of the base view is the one of the playlist that named the clip, as
  GetStereoscopicClip() gives it; the title information of libbluray is no longer read from
  the thread that demuxes.
- The log, at LOGINFO: the second view coming back, with how many pictures went without it;
  the clip being given up; the second view ending before the base view does, told apart from
  a read that failed; a play item without a second view, once when that begins, with the
  reason; the play item in the lines that speak of one; and what became of the last clip,
  which the destructor did not say.
